  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1255 AM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nThe main story over the next several days will be a major late\nseason heat wave, with the worst of the heat being Tuesday\nthrough Thursday. The driver of this heat will be a very strong\nH5 ridge that will expand from the southern Plains into the \nMid-South by Tuesday. \n\nUntil then, very warm temperatures and elevated humidity levels\nby mid-September standards are forecast today and Monday. High \ntemperatures will reach the lower to middle 90s and upper 80s to\nlower 90s today and Monday, respectively. Heat index values \nsouth of I-64 will be around 100 degrees. While just shy of Heat\nAdvisory criteria, given the late season nature of the\nconditions, did issue a Special Weather Statement through 23z \nfor areas south of I-64 highlighting the elevated heat \nconditions.\n\nA frontal boundary just the northwest will attempt to move into\nthe area today into Monday. This front may kick off a few stray\nshowers or thunderstorms (15-25%) in northern parts of southeast\nMO and locations north of I-64 today and tomorrow. Overall, the\ngreat majority of the region will stay dry, and locations that\ndo see rain will see 0.10\" or less on average. \n\nAs mentioned earlier, the worst of the heat looks to be Tuesday\nthrough Thursday. The intensity of the upper-level ridging is\nvery anomalous for mid-September. Ensemble guidance shows a\n596-599dm ridge (95-99th percentile) centered over the Mid-\nSouth during this time. Temperatures at 850 mb will range from\n19-22C which is also in the 95-99th percentiles. Actual air\ntemperatures will reach the middle to upper 90s (around record\nlevels). With dew point temperatures in the lower to middle \n70s, peak heat index values around 103-108F are expected. A Heat\nAdvisory will very likely be needed for most of all of the \nforecast area.\n\nBeyond Thursday, ensemble guidance has trended slower from\nprevious forecasts in breaking down the ridging over the region.\nThis means high temperatures will still be in the lower 90s on\nFriday and the upper 80s to lower 90s Saturday. However, it\nappears dew point temperatures will draw down into the 60s,\nwhich will keep heat index values in the 90s. A frontal boundary\nwill also move towards the area from the northwest, which will\nalso kick off a few showers and storms during the heat of the\nday Friday and Saturday.",
